[description]

[b]Gitea[/b] is a lightweight forge: Git hosting, issues, wiki, organizations, OAuth, and optional CI (“Actions”-compatible workflows) in a single Go binary that is easy to deploy behind nginx. [b]Forgejo[/b] forked from Gitea under community governance; both share similar UX and goals—pick based on governance, release cadence, and plugin ecosystem you trust.

[b]Why choose this family:[/b] minimal RAM footprint, fast setup for small teams, air-gapped labs, or personal servers. You trade fewer enterprise bells and whistles (advanced compliance modules, huge marketplace) for simplicity and cost.

[b]Git is unchanged:[/b] SSH and HTTP remotes behave like any other host; [code]git push[/code] updates refs server-side; PR/MR objects live in the forge database, not inside commit payloads.

[b]Organizations and teams:[/b] mirror GitHub/GitLab patterns at smaller scale—repos belong to users or orgs; team units grant access. Audit logging depth varies by version and configuration.

[b]Actions compatibility:[/b] when enabled, YAML workflows resemble GitHub Actions enough for many portable jobs, but always verify syntax and runner labels against your installed version’s docs.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] treat Gitea/Forgejo as “Git plus a thin collaboration shell”—ideal when you want ownership of the whole stack without running a heavyweight platform.
